      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the areas of the square and the circle, and then find the difference between these areas.\n\nFirst, let's find the side length of the square. The diagonal of the square is given as 5 inches. The relationship between the side length \\( s \\) of the square and its diagonal \\( d \\) is given by the Pythagorean theorem:\n\\[\nd = s\\sqrt{2}\n\\]\nGiven \\( d = 5 \\), we can solve for \\( s \\):\n\\[\n5 = s\\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\\[\ns = \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the area of the square. The area \\( A_{\\text{square}} \\) of a square with side length \\( s \\) is:\n\\[\nA_{\\text{square}} = s^2 = \\left(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)^2 = \\frac{25 \\cdot 2}{4} = \\frac{50}{4} = 12.5 \\text{ square inches}\n\\]\n\nNow, let's find the radius of the circle. The diameter of the circle is given as 5 inches, so the radius \\( r \\) is:\n\\[\nr = \\frac{5}{2} = 2.5 \\text{ inches}\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the area of the circle. The area \\( A_{\\text{circle}} \\) of a circle with radius \\( r \\) is:\n\\[\nA_{\\text{circle}} = \\pi r^2 = \\pi (2.5)^2 = \\pi \\cdot 6.25 = 6.25\\pi \\text{ square inches}\n\\]\n\nFinally, we find the difference between the area of the circle and the area of the square:\n\\[\nA_{\\text{circle}} - A_{\\text{square}} = 6.25\\pi - 12.5\n\\]\n\nUsing the approximation \\(\\pi \\approx 3.14159\\):\n\\[\n6.25\\pi \\approx 6.25 \\times 3.14159 \\approx 19.63496\n\\]\n\\[\nA_{\\text{circle}} - A_{\\text{square}} \\approx 19.63496 - 12.5 = 7.13496\n\\]\n\nRounding to the nearest tenth:\n\\[\n7.13496 \\approx 7.1\n\\]\n\nThus, the area of the circle is greater than the area of the square by \\(\\boxed{7.1}\\) square inches.",
