      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "First, we need to determine the side length of the square given its diagonal. Let \\( s \\) be the side length of the square. The relationship between the side length and the diagonal of a square is given by the Pythagorean theorem:\n\\[\ns\\sqrt{2} = 5\n\\]\nSolving for \\( s \\), we divide both sides by \\(\\sqrt{2}\\):\n\\[\ns = \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\]\nThe area of the square is then:\n\\[\ns^2 = \\left(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)^2 = \\frac{25 \\cdot 2}{4} = \\frac{50}{4} = 12.5 \\text{ square inches}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the area of the circle given its diameter. The radius \\( r \\) of the circle is half of the diameter:\n\\[\nr = \\frac{5}{2} = 2.5 \\text{ inches}\n\\]\nThe area of the circle is:\n\\[\n\\pi r^2 = \\pi (2.5)^2 = \\pi \\cdot 6.25 \\approx 19.625 \\text{ square inches}\n\\]\n\nTo find by how many square inches the area of the circle is greater than the area of the square, we subtract the area of the square from the area of the circle:\n\\[\n19.625 - 12.5 \\approx 7.125\n\\]\nRounding to the nearest tenth, we get:\n\\[\n\\boxed{7.1}\n\\]",
